<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Continuum — entitlements for the sideloaded build.

  Distribution is sideloading with custom signing, so these are simply present rather than
  requested: App Store review is out of scope.

  READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE JIT KEYS BELOW. `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` and
  `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` are macOS HARDENED RUNTIME keys. On
  iOS they are not the switch for executable memory and are simply ignored. iOS gates that behind
  `dynamic-codesigning`, which Apple restricts: NO provisioning profile grants it, development or
  distribution, free or paid, because it is not a capability a profile can carry. Only a signing
  bypass such as TrollStore, or a jailbreak, can.

  So "JIT is unconditional", which this comment used to claim, was wrong on the platform this
  file is for. Whether a recompiler can run is a property of HOW A COPY WAS INSTALLED and cannot
  be fixed from inside the repository. The button in Settings is what answers it on a given
  install; see `jit_probe.rs`.

  `dynamic-codesigning` is deliberately NOT added here. A restricted entitlement that the signing
  identity cannot carry can make an install fail outright, and an app that will not install is a
  worse outcome than one that cannot recompile. Add it only against a signing method that is
  known to grant it.

  The two memory entitlements do different jobs and both are needed:

    increased-memory-limit        raises the jetsam cap — resident, dirty pages. This is
                                 what stops the app being killed for the memory it
                                 actually touches.
    extended-virtual-addressing  raises the virtual address space beyond 4 GB. This is
                                 what allows the large sparse reservation every dynarec
                                 wants for fast-mem: a PROT_NONE reservation has no
                                 resident pages, so it costs address space and nothing
                                 else, and does not affect jetsam risk at all.

  Conflating them is the easy mistake. Without the first, a Switch title is killed for
  using 6 GB. Without the second, the reservation fails before it starts.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- JIT. mmap with MAP_JIT, then pthread_jit_write_protect_np per thread, then
	     sys_icache_invalidate — which is NOT optional on arm64: omitting it works in the
	     simulator and crashes on device, intermittently. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Some recompilers map a page W and X simultaneously rather than toggling. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>

	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>

	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Debug builds get a further raise, which is worth having while measuring a Switch
	     title's real footprint before deciding what to gate on. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-debugging-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
